          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Let’s have a look at the different ways that data can be stored.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> External Hard drives can be used to store copies of important files. The benefit for this is that they are portable and sturdy and extend the storage capacity of a computer. They should always be encrypted especially when sensitive data is stored.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Cloud storage is becoming more popular for safe storage of files and can be accessible from any device which has secured access to it. We all use cloud storage everyday as many emails are stored in the cloud. Cloud storage means data is kept separate from a user’s location and can be available 24/7 allowing organisations extra storage and web services without investing in expensive hardware up front.</p>
<p>It’s important to ensure that any cloud storage has the right access controls because it’s so highly available over the internet that only the right people have permission to access it.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Flash drives are useful to keep data to hand, but they are easy to lose. Flash Drives should always be encrypted, and password protected, especially when storing sensitive data.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Back ups are copies of the system or network data for file restoration or archiving. They are an essential part of a ‘business continuity’ plan for data protection and recovery. Business critical data should always be backed up regularly along with other key software components of a system. This not only stops opportunities for blackmail by ransomware, it also protects from the impact of fire, flood or physical damage or theft. Data backups should not be accessible to staff or permanently connected to the same network as the original over a LAN to stop them being infected by Ransomware and other malware, should that network become compromised. You wouldn’t want a breach to take out both the live data and your backups as well. It’s also important to remember to ensure that your backups have the same or more stringent access controls on as the live data.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Passwords are strings of characters that verify the identity of users during the authentication process. Some people use the same password all the time, which is not good! This means that if one password is found out, criminals will subsequently know the passwords to all your accounts. Some have too many and can't remember them all so write them down! Also, not good. Passwords need to be changed regularly and should include different characters such as numbers, caps and lower-case letters and other characters. Password managers are a good way of ensuring that you can have complex and unique passwords for every website without having to remember them all. The longer the password is the better as it is harder to crack. Passwords can be made more secure by processes like 'hashing' and 'salting' which puts them through an algorithm that turns plain text into an unintelligible series of letters and numbers so that if a password is breached, the criminal is unable to find out what your actual password is.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> To prevent 'malware' from affecting a system and to protect data, antivirus software also known as anti-malware should be used and enabled on all computers and systems. These should always be kept up to date to cover the latest threats.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Operating systems should also be kept up to date and often include antivirus software and firewalls which create a 'buffer zone' between a network and external networks such as the internet. Operating systems should be set to 'automatically update' and when updates are no longer available, operating systems may need to be changed to a newer, more current OS or even a brand new computer system.</p>
<p><strong>Nicole:</strong> Share level permissions manage access to folders shared over a network and don't apply to users who log on locally, whereas file level permissions grant access directly to individuals’ objects such as files, folders, or hard disks on servers. Share permissions should be granted on a role-based access control [RBAC] model. This means ensuring that people are granted access to those shared files depending on what their role is. For example, only people in the Finance Department should be able to access the shared space containing the finance documents. Files within a given shared space should also be further restricted. For example, within the finance department, perhaps only the payroll people should have access to salary information.</p>
<p><strong>Abraham:</strong> Encryption is a type of cryptography where a message is encrypted so that only</p>
<p>authorised parties can read it, converting plain text into an unintelligible series of letters and numbers. Encryption is crucial in helping protect against bad actors from around the world. If all information was plain text sensitive materials like passwords and health records and bank account information could be stolen and sold to the highest bidder. Encryption works by granting a pair of keys that allows only the holders of the keys to decrypt and read the data. It’s therefore incredibly important to keep control over who has these keys.</p>

